00:41:44,800 --> 00:41:52,631
So the way that you know is we have a kind of a a transfer of responsibility mechanism. We have a model for that.

368
00:41:52,640 --> 00:42:04,150
It's like a big thing that we we can install in big organizations, but we kind of created a little slice of it, a sliced down version of it just for teams to use in RMF.

369
00:42:04,150 --> 00:42:10,550
And so you know that you have shared understanding when you meet the standard

370
00:42:10,550 --> 00:42:19,829
that the team is willing to put themselves completely in the product or stakeholders hands like be at their mercy for when the work item closes.

371
00:42:19,839 --> 00:42:23,349
That's kind of one of the main standards.

372
00:42:23,349 --> 00:42:25,510
So what we say is responsibility starts off with product.

373
00:42:25,520 --> 00:42:30,309
It starts off with business. It starts off with the stakeholders or whatever, right?

374
00:42:30,309 --> 00:42:40,470
Eventually, responsibility for the work item is going to transfer into the team at the end of a cycle or or whenever it is they go to close that work item.

375
00:42:40,470 --> 00:42:44,870
Responsibility is going to need to be taken back by product.

376
00:42:44,870 --> 00:42:54,790
And product isn't going to do that unless it actually meets the standard that they had envisioned.

377
00:42:54,790 --> 00:43:07,349
And so the team needs to be sure that their understanding that their envisioning of the output of that work item is the same as product's envisioning of the output of that work item.

378
00:43:07,349 --> 00:43:11,750
And that way when it's time to close the work item, it will run smoothly.

379
00:43:11,750 --> 00:43:16,309
If the team isn't sure of that, then the team will keep saying, "No, this is not ready.

380
00:43:16,309 --> 00:43:24,630
We don't have the understanding." Now, I I'm not going to pretend that just saying that like there's a magic wand where you say that and it's true.

381
00:43:24,630 --> 00:43:30,710
You have to sometimes for some teams, you have to go through some kind of like painful retraining cycles where they say, "Okay, yeah, sure.

382
00:43:30,710 --> 00:43:39,990
We understand." They wave their hands and then we get to the end of the sprint or or whatever, you know, whatever the end time marker is and they say, "Okay, well, we did it."

383
00:43:39,990 --> 00:43:47,829
you know, and and then product says, "Yeah, but you didn't do what I asked, and so I'm not going to give you credit or I'm not going to close it or I'm not going to let us move on to the next thing.

384
00:43:47,829 --> 00:43:57,430
I'm going to make you go back and do it again." And that happens in a visible way where it's clear that the team didn't do what was requested of them by product.

385
00:43:57,430 --> 00:44:03,589
And after one or two of those cycles, then the team gets it.

386
00:44:03,589 --> 00:44:12,550
When I say I understand, I have to be so confident that I understand that I'll be held in the crucible of that understanding at the end of this story.

387
00:44:12,550 --> 00:44:24,069
And you know depending on the the organization that I'm in the consequences of saying I understood when I didn't really understand of not faring out hidden assumptions and whatnot.

388
00:44:24,069 --> 00:44:35,270
Those will range between being a little embarrassed and having egg on your face versus you know in some really like toxic organizations maybe you get castigated or something like that but there's some kind of skin in the game.

389
00:44:35,270 --> 00:44:38,790
Something is on the line if they don't have that understanding.

390
00:44:38,800 --> 00:44:41,191
Right. Got it.

391
00:44:41,191 --> 00:44:45,190
So we'll move to the next section where we'll double click on definition of done.

392
00:44:45,190 --> 00:44:49,910
So I understand you have developed a bespoke definition of done.

393
00:44:49,910 --> 00:44:56,390
How is that different from the normal definition of done which is used at the project level and it's standard across the teams right?

394
00:44:56,390 --> 00:45:02,230
All the tasks use it and we just make sure that it fulfills those criteria which I mentioned over there.

395
00:45:02,230 --> 00:45:05,270
Why did you feel the need to create a bespoke definition of done?

396
00:45:05,280 --> 00:45:10,470
Sure.

397
00:45:10,470 --> 00:45:24,710
So working with teams that have a definition of done that they've already come up with the typical thing that I've seen with teams is that they would have in the beginning when they adopted scrum or they went through scrum training or something like that they were told to come up

398
00:45:24,710 --> 00:45:34,069
with a definition of done and so they might have spent some time doing that but what is typically being taught in the industry is

399
00:45:34,069 --> 00:45:46,470
that the team has a definition of done of what it typically means for that work item or for a implementation item to be done and they would have a lot of general things in there.

400
00:45:46,470 --> 00:45:48,630
You know, we need to like coding standards and testing standards.

401
00:45:48,630 --> 00:45:58,870
The code had to have been code reviewed by, you know, a non-author might have had to go through some architectural review, things like that.

402
00:45:58,880 --> 00:46:10,309
And these are great as far as making sure that you're capturing your standards, your development standards, both coding and architectural and testing standards around that.

403
00:46:10,309 --> 00:46:21,430
The only problem is that what I have found in general is once teams have created that definition of done, it's a generic definition of done.

404
00:46:21,430 --> 00:46:25,990
That's kind of a catch all for all the different types of work items that they take on.

405
00:46:25,990 --> 00:46:29,910
Maybe in the beginning because they only did it the first time and never revisited it.

406
00:46:29,920 --> 00:46:41,589
It only contains a part of the criteria that they normally would have to take on, especially as their work evolved over time.

407
00:46:41,589 --> 00:46:45,829
But it's the typically it's the type of thing that is created and then just forgotten.

408
00:46:45,829 --> 00:46:52,790
So in so many cases, in fact the vast majority of cases when I would work with a team, I would ask them, so do you have a definition of done?

409
00:46:52,790 --> 00:46:54,630
And half the team wouldn't even know that they had one.

410
00:46:54,640 --> 00:47:05,430
And some people who might have been there from the beginning would say, "Yeah, we have a definition of done." And I I would ask them, "Well, where is it?" And then the half of those people might not even know where it is.

411
00:47:05,430 --> 00:47:09,349
And somebody would find out and they say, "Okay, here's where it is and we'll take a look at it."

412
00:47:09,349 --> 00:47:20,790
And then I would ask them, "Have you actually been looking at this definition of done at the end of the sprint and making sure that every work item actually adheres to this definition of done?"

413
00:47:20,790 --> 00:47:26,390
And then the answer would usually be no. And then the question would lead to well why not?

414
00:47:26,400 --> 00:47:36,470
Well because it's because it's not most of those things don't apply to this particular work item or something like that or you know we've forgotten.

415
00:47:36,470 --> 00:47:40,950
So the problem with that type of thing is that it's like almost a safety blanket.

416
00:47:40,950 --> 00:47:44,230
We can say that we have it but we don't really use it.

417
00:47:44,230 --> 00:47:52,230
It doesn't really serve a true purpose other than just making us feel warm and fuzzy that we've actually created this thing that half the people don't even know exists anymore.

418
00:47:52,230 --> 00:47:58,630
The problem is is that each work item itself is typically different.

419
00:47:58,630 --> 00:48:06,870
Even in teams that do similar work and work on the same systems over and over and adding maybe making some small changes to the same piece of functionality over

420
00:48:06,870 --> 00:48:14,710
and over, many cases those work items are different in shape and nature. And so how many requirements do you have?

421
00:48:14,710 --> 00:48:16,550
What are the acceptance criteria in there?

422
00:48:16,550 --> 00:48:19,270
Do you need to create documentation?

423
00:48:19,270 --> 00:48:23,829
Do you truly need to actually go and do a an architectural review?

424
00:48:23,829 --> 00:48:28,790
Is there something that we need to do that goes beyond just this discussion?

425
00:48:28,790 --> 00:48:30,630
Do we need to do something with a support team?

426
00:48:30,630 --> 00:48:35,829
Do we need to have user acceptance done on that?

427
00:48:35,829 --> 00:48:38,950
Do we need to have legal review of that, etc., etc.?

428
00:48:38,950 --> 00:48:50,710
When you take a look at a typical team's backlog of like the last 100 product backlog items or stories that they've developed and you look across them and you really dig into the details of each one of them,

429
00:48:50,710 --> 00:48:59,030
you'll understand that the true definition of done is so vastly different in many cases across

430
00:48:59,030 --> 00:49:08,310
that backlog of work items that the original definition of done that people have kind of internalized or maybe not even remember just doesn't even apply. apply to it.

431
00:49:08,310 --> 00:49:09,910
It might have too many things.

432
00:49:09,910 --> 00:49:12,470
It might not have enough things in it.

433
00:49:12,470 --> 00:49:19,030
And therein lies again the reason why teams end up failing in actually completing the work items

434
00:49:19,030 --> 00:49:27,829
that they've been asked to implement because they don't really understand what done means for that particular item.

435
00:49:27,829 --> 00:49:37,109
So that's the one part of it and the other part of it is that you know most of the time scrum teams are asked to estimate the size you know the effort

436
00:49:37,109 --> 00:49:52,069
for that work item and what I've realized over time is that teams don't really succeed in providing a reliable estimate if they don't actually understand what done means for that specific work item.

437
00:49:52,069 --> 00:50:01,349
So sitting down with a work item then and actually discussing and then eventually codifying you know defining what done really means

438
00:50:01,349 --> 00:50:09,030
for this work item gives the team a much greater clarity on what the actual work is going

439
00:50:09,040 --> 00:50:18,950
to be that they need to go and do not just from an implementation perspective but also what other things need to be done as part of this.

440
00:50:18,950 --> 00:50:20,870
Do we need to create documentation?

441
00:50:20,870 --> 00:50:23,190
Do we need to go and have a review with legal etc. Right?

442
00:50:23,200 --> 00:50:30,390
And that creates the type of clarity for a team to truly understand and be able to succeed with that specific work item.

443
00:50:30,400 --> 00:50:44,950
So it's really about trying to take every single work item and treating it as if it is a special case of its own and making sure that we have truly captured what done means for that work item.

444
00:50:44,950 --> 00:50:53,349
And over time we'll find that there are kind of categories of work items that have a similar shape and size in terms of the definition of done and what needs to be done.

445
00:50:53,349 --> 00:50:55,349
So not all work is the same, right?

446
00:50:55,349 --> 00:50:57,190
So sometimes there's database work that needs to be done.

447
00:50:57,200 --> 00:51:08,309
Sometimes there's I guess an API that needs to be created that's different from or making changes to an API which is potentially different from making changes to the user interface,

448
00:51:08,309 --> 00:51:16,069
graphical design of that, etc. And so these types of work items can mean different things that need to be done for that work item.

449
00:51:16,079 --> 00:51:23,589
And so as you start seeing that there are these categories then you can say okay well this type of work item is about like that.

450
00:51:23,589 --> 00:51:29,589
And so that's what leads us to also sometimes creating different templates for definition of done with different type of work items.

451
00:51:29,599 --> 00:51:39,670
In every case, we want to make sure that the team has a precise and accurate understanding

452
00:51:39,670 --> 00:51:45,750
and documentation of what done will mean for that work item and not just assume that we understand what it is.

453
00:51:45,750 --> 00:51:53,750
It's like giving them the ability to at the end to certify we have done everything that we have agreed upon from the beginning before we started implementing

454
00:51:53,750 --> 00:52:02,710
that needed to have been done to be able to call this thing done and say we have implemented it and that behavior

455
00:52:02,710 --> 00:52:17,589
as well as this you know not just the documentation but the the doing of this and certifying of that at the end creates a level of trust and acceleration in teams that is unlike What

456
00:52:17,589 --> 00:52:26,390
I see in teams not doing this the challenge is you know you've got various types of programmers you've got junior programmers people have just started off and you've got programmers

457
00:52:26,400 --> 00:52:41,270
who are lazy and what stops them then because it's been say an organizational mandate or it's a mandate for that particular program that you need to have a specific definition of done for each work item.

458
00:52:41,270 --> 00:52:49,349
What stops them from just doing a copy paste for you know and not making it specific for that task or that work item?

459
00:52:49,349 --> 00:52:57,589
What do you look for in that definition of done to ensure it's a good quality one which will ensure that it is can be seen as sign off.

460
00:52:57,599 --> 00:53:02,150
So it comes down to responsibility in my opinion.

461
00:53:02,150 --> 00:53:05,589
The definition of done isn't just signed off by one party.

462
00:53:05,589 --> 00:53:14,790
The definition of done has to be approved by everyone and everyone approving it becomes responsible for it.

463
00:53:14,790 --> 00:53:19,349
So the agreement manifest in the definition of done is

464
00:53:19,349 --> 00:53:31,349
that when all of these items are truly met, this work item will close and responsibility for it will transfer from the team back to you know whoever is in charge of product, product owner, product manager, whatever, right?

465
00:53:31,349 --> 00:53:40,309
The product manager then has an impetus to ensure that there are no, for lack of a better term, loopholes, right?

466
00:53:40,309 --> 00:53:45,589
That that definition of done is the right definition of done for that work item.

467
00:53:45,599 --> 00:53:56,710
So that when it comes back to them, they don't get to point the figure finger at the team and say they did it wrong because the definition of done is what the team was supposed to do and the team did the definition of done, right?

468
00:53:56,710 --> 00:54:03,589
So once they've accepted it, if something's wrong, they're going to be the ones that are on the hook for being wrong.

469
00:54:03,589 --> 00:54:09,990
So they're going to push back on accepting it on the basis of that definition of done.

470
00:54:09,990 --> 00:54:12,230
But they can't push back in this process.

471
00:54:12,230 --> 00:54:17,990
They can't push back on the basis of like, oh, I forgot something or whatever.

472
00:54:17,990 --> 00:54:23,670
So they've got a a big driver to make sure that definition of done is right.

473
00:54:23,670 --> 00:54:26,069
The team has a similar driver, right?

474
00:54:26,069 --> 00:54:39,670
They want to make sure that the definition of done is exactly right so that they can use it to implement things in a way that will get the work item to be accepted and closed and move forward.

475
00:54:39,680 --> 00:54:53,829
And they're going to have some pressure if if they're really lazy, which I I mean I don't know how many teams are really lazy, but if there is a team that's really lazy, they'll have pressure from outside that says, "Well, you've also got to have the architecture review.

476
00:54:53,829 --> 00:54:55,510
You've got to have all these things, right?

477
00:54:55,510 --> 00:55:00,390
You've got to have all of these standards be met in order for this work item to be closed.

478
00:55:00,390 --> 00:55:04,630
And that way we keep our code quality higher and we can maintain the product over time.

479
00:55:04,630 --> 00:55:08,630
So, it's the tension between how do I get work started on it?

480
00:55:08,630 --> 00:55:16,151
Well, getting work started on it means everybody's agreed on a work item or on the definition of done. So, that's one pressure.

481
00:55:16,151 --> 00:55:20,950
And that pressure makes it so that people don't try to like throw everything in the kitchen sink in it.

482
00:55:20,950 --> 00:55:22,870
They try to make it just right for just that item.

483
00:55:22,870 --> 00:55:26,710
And then the other pressure is how are we going to get this handed off at the end.

484
00:55:26,710 --> 00:55:39,190
And that makes sure that everybody has what they need in order to be able to later be held to account and kind of pass that test for that work item in it.

485
00:55:39,190 --> 00:55:42,790
It's the tension between those two forces, right?

486
00:55:42,790 --> 00:55:51,349
And this transfer of responsibility is what stops product owners saying that you know I thought this was an implicit requirement in the early example you had given.

487
00:55:51,349 --> 00:55:53,910
This is where it you know gets caught.

488
00:55:53,910 --> 00:55:57,030
Nothing actually stops them from saying it right.

489
00:55:57,030 --> 00:55:59,349
All you can do is create consequences when they do say it.

490
00:55:59,349 --> 00:56:04,150
So when the team has an agreement and presumably management is in on this agreement.

491
00:56:04,150 --> 00:56:06,150
This isn't something you can do in a really grassroots way.

492
00:56:06,150 --> 00:56:08,069
You've got to have management involved.

493
00:56:08,069 --> 00:56:15,670
You might even need leadership involved and people from other parts of the organization other than engineering involved and on board.

494
00:56:15,670 --> 00:56:20,549
Once you have that kind of alignment and agreement, this is how we're going to do business.

495
00:56:20,559 --> 00:56:27,109
Then I guess if a product owner were to say, oh, I forgot this piece, so we need to do more.

496
00:56:27,109 --> 00:56:31,430
It's the team's responsibility to say that's not how this works.

497
00:56:31,430 --> 00:56:39,109
And then you know I guess if the product owner is really stubborn and holding it open or whatever then there'd be some sort of escalation process where you get intervention from management.

498
00:56:39,109 --> 00:56:46,549
I really don't find that to be a very common problem because I mean initially like in the training phase it might be but everybody wants to do a good job.

499
00:56:46,559 --> 00:56:51,430
Everybody wants things to go smoothly and this makes things go smoothly and gets us a better job.

500
00:56:51,440 --> 00:56:54,309
Right. Got it. Lunal you had something to add.

501
00:56:54,319 --> 00:56:56,870
Yes.

502
00:56:56,870 --> 00:57:05,430
So like Max actually just said now is most of the times people are trying to do the right thing but what this gives us is two things.

503
00:57:05,430 --> 00:57:12,309
One so in addition to helping us solve a dysfunctional environment

504
00:57:12,309 --> 00:57:22,150
where people are not taking responsibility or they're being lazy or they have poor practices or something like that.

505
00:57:22,150 --> 00:57:31,430
It also helps in scenarios where teams are truly highly successful and highly capable, highly motivated but just want to make sure that the right thing gets done.

506
00:57:31,430 --> 00:57:39,270
And one of the examples that I like to or one of the things I guess mechanisms I want to talk about is

507
00:57:39,270 --> 00:57:48,630
that in scrum a good product owner understands that they are ultimately responsible for what the scrum team develops.

508
00:57:48,630 --> 00:57:56,230
They have a a fiduciary responsibility to the organization to essentially put the right things into the team's sprints,

509
00:57:56,240 --> 00:58:05,030
the right work items, and make sure that the team ultimately is producing the value that the company is paying for, right?

510
00:58:05,030 --> 00:58:07,510
Because the company is paying a certain amount per sprint, if you will.

511
00:58:07,520 --> 00:58:18,230
And that goes, you know, for how much they're paying for the facilities, but they're paying for salaries of the people involved, the product owner, the scrum master, the scrum team, etc. Right?

512
00:58:18,230 --> 00:58:21,430
And so as the product owner, you are kind of like a mini CEO almost.

513
00:58:21,430 --> 00:58:32,309
You have this fiduciary responsibility to make sure that the team is working on the right things and are producing the right things to create the value that the company is paying for.

514
00:58:32,309 --> 00:58:42,309
So as a product owner myself, I want to make sure that I am giving the team the best chance of succeeding in delivering this thing that I'm asking them to deliver.

515
00:58:42,319 --> 00:58:50,549
So having clarity on the definition of done, what it actually means to be done is not just important for the team.

516
00:58:50,549 --> 00:58:58,230
It's important for me because I need to make sure that the team has this clear understanding of what done actually means.

517
00:58:58,230 --> 00:59:01,589
But it also means I have to have a clear understanding of what done means.

518
00:59:01,589 --> 00:59:07,750
And if I didn't think about all these other things that needed to be done, then I I might be setting the team up for failure.

519
00:59:07,750 --> 00:59:13,030
If I don't get an opportunity for the team to point out the additional things that need to be done, I'm setting them up for failure.

520
00:59:13,040 --> 00:59:23,510
If I don't give them the ability to actually create a or to apply a reliable estimate for instance for the work of what they think is going

521
00:59:23,510 --> 00:59:32,069
to take in this sprint and one that they can feel absolutely that they can stand by and that they comfortable with then I'm again I'm setting them up for failure.

522
00:59:32,079 --> 00:59:38,710
So it works both ways and so yes this handoff of responsibility is an absolute forcing function to help solve problems

523
00:59:38,720 --> 00:59:48,069
when we have dysfunction in that but it also helps us create the guard rails that even a high performing team needs

524
00:59:48,079 --> 00:59:57,190
to have to make sure that what's being asked for is clear that both parties agree to it and that we can actually succeed in delivering that at the end.

525
00:59:57,200 --> 01:00:03,190
Would you be able to explain what the definition of ready is and why it needs to be bespoke?

526
01:00:03,200 --> 01:00:16,950
It's similar to the definition of done wherein you know each work item is different or should be treated as potentially different from other work items.

527
01:00:16,950 --> 01:00:25,109
But an example of definition of ready we we typically have two categories in our definition of ready and that is product exit criteria.

528
01:00:25,109 --> 01:00:32,710
In other words, what are the criteria that needs to be met in order for product management,

529
01:00:32,710 --> 01:00:40,710
your product owner, product manager to be able to say this work item and everything in it, the requirements, you know,

530
01:00:40,710 --> 01:00:49,510
the specifications that are listed in there and everything that is that is needed to be provided to the team are ready.

531
01:00:49,510 --> 01:00:52,150
So, you know, everything is ready.

532
01:00:52,150 --> 01:00:54,549
So, an example would be not just the requirements itself.

533
01:00:54,549 --> 01:01:04,309
So, if I was to do behavior-driven development, there would be behavioral specifications for all of the different scenarios that are in scope for this work item.

534
01:01:04,309 --> 01:01:11,270
But there might be things there that I have to provide the team such as UI design or creative designs, right?

535
01:01:11,270 --> 01:01:13,670
There might be a UI mockup or something like that.

536
01:01:13,680 --> 01:01:20,309
There might be additional information that I have to provide in addition to just the actual kind of specifications.

537
01:01:20,319 --> 01:01:31,670
So I need to I need to make sure that when I hand this requirement off to the team

538
01:01:31,670 --> 01:01:45,031
to implement then they have I am comfortable that I've given them everything that is needed and I have to be comfortable that the requirement itself is actually representative of the stakeholders expectations. Right?

539
01:01:45,031 --> 01:01:51,599
So I might have had some specifications there that are not fully baked yet and I still need to go
